METALPERFTRACE(1)           General Commands Manual          METALPERFTRACE(1)

NAME
     metalperftrace – collect and inspect Metal GPU performance data

SYNOPSIS
     metalperftrace subcommand [options]

     metalperftrace collect [--last duration | --start date --end date]
                    [--prefix prefix] [--json] output_dir

     metalperftrace overview [--json] [--json-include-timeline]
                    [--include-state-transitions] [--predicate expr]
                    [--aggregate] [--start offset] [--end offset]
                    [--domain domain] [--state-label label] trace_file

     metalperftrace listen [--json] [--interval sec] [--output file]
                    [--process name] [--pid pid] [--predicate expr]

     metalperftrace setup --enable feature | --disable feature [--pid pid]

DESCRIPTION
     metalperftrace collects and inspects always-on recorded Metal graphics
     performance data.  The system continuously records frame timing and
     related statistics for all Metal layers.  This tool retrieves that data
     and either stores it into a trace file that can be opened by tools such
     as Instruments, or streams it live as human-readable text or JSON.

     The following subcommands are available:

     collect   Retrieve always-on recorded Metal performance metrics for a
               given time range and write them as .atrc files into output_dir
               (created if it does not exist).  Re-running the same command
               with the same directory skips files that were already
               collected.

     overview  Print a performance summary from a previously collected .atrc
               trace file.  By default, prints an aggregation over the entire
               trace duration.

     listen    Stream live Metal performance statistics as data arrives from
               the system, refreshing on a configurable interval.

     setup     Dynamically enable or disable a Metal performance trace feature
               in a running Metal process.

SUBCOMMAND: COLLECT
   Synopsis
     metalperftrace collect [--last duration | --start date --end date]
     [--prefix prefix] [--json] output_dir

   Options
     --last duration
                  Collect trace data from the last duration.  The suffix
                  determines the unit: ‘s’ (seconds, default), ‘m’ (minutes),
                  ‘h’ (hours), ‘d’ (days).  A bare number without a suffix is
                  treated as seconds.  Examples: 30s, 5m, 2h, 1d.  Mutually
                  exclusive with --start.

     --start date
                  Start of the collection window (absolute date).  Accepted
                  formats: yyyy-MM-dd, yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss,
                  yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ssZ, @unix_timestamp.  Dates in the future
                  are not allowed.

     --end date   End of the collection window.  Same format as --start.
                  Defaults to the current time when omitted.

     --prefix prefix
                  File name prefix for the collected trace files.  Defaults to
                  MetalPerfTrace.

     --json       Output collected file paths as a JSON dictionary with a
                  traces key.  Enabled by default when stdout is not a
                  terminal.

SUBCOMMAND: OVERVIEW
   Synopsis
     metalperftrace overview [--json] [--json-include-timeline]
     [--include-state-transitions] [--predicate expr] [--aggregate] [--start
     offset] [--end offset] [--domain domain] [--state-label label] trace_file

   Description
     Print per-process, per-layer performance metrics from a collected trace.
     By default, prints an aggregation over the entire trace duration with
     full detail for all metric sections.

   Options
     --json                      Emit output as a JSON object instead of
                                 human-readable text.  Contains aggregated
                                 summary statistics for each layer session.

     --json-include-timeline     Include the full per-frame timeline data in
                                 JSON output.  Implies --json.

     --include-state-transitions
                                 Show detailed StateReporter state transition
                                 history, including labels, time ranges, and
                                 stable state values for each domain.

     --predicate expr            An NSPredicate expression used to filter the
                                 layers shown.  The expression is evaluated
                                 against a dictionary with keys: processName
                                 (string), pid (integer), layerName (string).

     --aggregate                 Enable aggregation mode.  Groups metrics by
                                 state transitions.  Without additional
                                 options, aggregates over all {domain, label,
                                 stableState} keys across all domains.

     --start offset              Start offset from trace start (used with
                                 --aggregate).  Duration format, e.g. 5s, 2m,
                                 1h.

     --end offset                End offset from trace start (used with
                                 --aggregate).  Defaults to trace end when
                                 omitted.

     --domain domain             Restrict aggregation to a single state domain
                                 (used with --aggregate).

     --state-label label         With --domain, group by {domain, label} only,
                                 ignoring stableState differences.  Requires
                                 --domain.

SUBCOMMAND: LISTEN
   Synopsis
     metalperftrace listen [--json] [--interval sec] [--output file]
     [--process name] [--pid pid] [--predicate expr]

   Description
     Stream live Metal performance statistics as data arrives from the system.
     Always prints full detail for all metric sections.

   Options
     --json         Emit each update as a compact JSON object on a single line
                    (NDJSON).  When the session ends (via SIGINT, SIGTERM, or
                    SIGHUP), the output file, if any, is rewritten as a single
                    pretty-printed JSON array.

     --interval sec, -i sec
                    Update interval in seconds.  Minimum: 1.0.  Default: 1.0.

     --output file, -o file
                    Tee all output to file in addition to the terminal.  The
                    file must not already exist.

     --process name, -p name
                    Restrict output to the named process.  May be repeated.

     --pid pid      Restrict output to the given process ID.  May be repeated.

     --predicate expr
                    Same as for the overview subcommand.

SUBCOMMAND: SETUP
   Synopsis
     metalperftrace setup --enable feature | --disable feature [--pid pid]

   Description
     Enable or disable a Metal performance trace feature in one or more
     running Metal processes.  At least one --pid option is required.  Exactly
     one of --enable or --disable must be specified.

   Features
     per-frame-metrics        Per-frame signpost collection (per-drawable
                              signposting).  Equivalent to setting
                              METAL_PERFORMANCE_PER_FRAME_SIGNPOSTS=1 at
                              launch.

     shader-compiler-metrics  Shader compiler statistics signpost collection.
                              Equivalent to setting
                              METAL_PERFORMANCE_SHADER_COMPILER_STATS_SIGNPOSTS=1
                              at launch.

     hud                      Metal performance HUD overlay.

   Options
     --enable feature
               Enable the named feature.

     --disable feature
               Disable the named feature.

     --pid pid
               Target the process with the given PID.  May be repeated.

EXIT STATUS
     The metalperftrace utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

EXAMPLES
     Collect the last 30 minutes of trace data:

           metalperftrace collect --last 30m /tmp/traces

     Print a text overview from a collected trace:

           metalperftrace overview /tmp/traces/trace.atrc

     Export a JSON summary:

           metalperftrace overview --json /tmp/traces/trace.atrc

     Export JSON with full per-frame timeline:

           metalperftrace overview --json-include-timeline
           /tmp/traces/trace.atrc

     Aggregate metrics by state domain:

           metalperftrace overview --aggregate --domain com.example.App
           /tmp/traces/trace.atrc

     Aggregate for a specific time window:

           metalperftrace overview --aggregate --start 5m --end 10m
           /tmp/traces/trace.atrc

     Stream live stats filtered to one process:

           metalperftrace listen --process MyGame --interval 2

     Stream live stats in JSON mode with file logging:

           metalperftrace listen --json --output /tmp/session.json

     Enable per-frame metrics in a running process:

           metalperftrace setup --enable per-frame-metrics --pid 1234

SEE ALSO
     trace(1), xctrace(1)

